<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hildesheim</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hildesheim is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> founded its bishopric in 815, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Bernward around 1000 it became a centre of Ottonian art; its cathedral and St. Michael's Church are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The prince-bishops ruled a territory until 1803, after which it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and in 1815 to the Kingdom of Hanover.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hildesheim.jpg" alt="Hildesheim" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tilman2007 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hildesheim,_Dom_20171201_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hildesheim lies on the Innerste river, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a>. According to tradition the city was named after its founder Hildwin. It may have been founded when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> moved from Elze to the ford across the Innerste, an important market on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellweg">Hellweg</a> trade route. The settlement around the cathedral grew quickly into a town, and King Otto III granted it market rights in 983. The present market place was laid out at the beginning of the 13th century, when the city had about 5,000 inhabitants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hildesheim obtained city status in 1249 and was then one of the biggest cities in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The clergy ruled it for four centuries, until a town hall was built, work on the present one beginning in 1268. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> in 1367. Most of its inhabitants became Lutherans in 1542, and during the Thirty Years' War it was besieged and occupied several times. The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, to which it belonged, was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866.</p><p>On 22 March 1945 British air raids bombed the historic centre, which had kept its medieval character, and almost levelled it. Some 29 per cent of the houses were destroyed and 45 per cent damaged, and around 200 of 800 half-timbered houses survived. Most of the major churches were soon rebuilt in their original style, and reconstruction of the historic centre began in the 1980s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Romanesque cathedral, with its ancient bronze doors made around 1015, and St. Michael's Church, an early Romanesque church of Ottonian architecture built from 1010 to 1022, are both on the UNESCO World Heritage list. A rose bush on the wall of the cathedral apse, reputed to be 1,000 years old, still flowers. The market place was reconstructed in 1984–90, and the University of Hildesheim was founded in 1978.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hildesheim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saxony was then governed by Frankish counts and bishops, and Christianity was spread through new sees such as Paderborn, Bremen and <b>Hildesheim</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Essen Abbey was founded around 845 to 852 by Altfrid, later bishop of <b>Hildesheim</b>, as a community of canonesses for women of the Saxon nobility.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-essen">Imperial Abbey of Essen</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress of Vienna raised the Electorate of Hanover to a kingdom in 1814, with George III as king, and enlarged it with East Frisia, <b>Hildesheim</b> and Lingen.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hanover">Kingdom of Hanover</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis the Pious founded the diocese of <b>Hildesheim</b> in 815.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-hildesheim">Prince-Bishopric of Hildesheim</a></li></ul></section>
